\section{Method}

\subsection{Task Definition}

We study \emph{verdict prediction}: given a Python function, a test input, and an optional execution trace, predict whether the test passes or fails.
The model produces a single token (\texttt{pass} or \texttt{fail}), making this a binary classification task.
Unlike code generation \citep{chen2025tracepile} or output prediction \citep{gu2024cruxeval}, verdict prediction isolates the model's ability to detect faults from its ability to simulate execution.
The discrete binary outcome makes the effect of each trace component directly measurable, providing cleaner ablation contrasts than generation tasks where output quality varies continuously.

\subsection{Dataset}
\label{sec:dataset}

We sampled 10 candidate solutions per problem from Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct (temperature 0.8, top\_p 0.95) for 591 problems from HumanEval \citep{chen2021evaluating} (164) and MBPP-sanitized \citep{austin2021program} (427), executed each against canonical tests with \texttt{sys.settrace} to obtain line-level traces and pass/fail verdicts, yielding 5,910 pairs (67\% pass, 33\% fail).
The dataset is split 80/20 by problem ID into 4,720 training and 1,190 test samples (majority baseline: 71.51\%).
The \texttt{exception\_only} format collapses passing solutions into identical rows (39.45\% train, 48.99\% test duplicates); these duplicates arise from multiple candidate solutions per problem, with the train--test split enforced at the problem level (472 train / 119 test problems, zero crossover; Appendix~\ref{sec:dedup_stats}).

\subsection{Trace Representations}

We define seven conditions that vary the trace information available (Figure~\ref{fig:trace_overview}):
\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{no\_trace}: Source code and test input only.
\item \texttt{full\_trace}: Complete line-by-line trace with variable states.
\item \texttt{VOTA}: Exception events + assertion-point comparisons + loop compression (Appendix~\ref{sec:vota_algorithm}).
\item \texttt{exception\_only}: Only exception/error events.
\item \texttt{ae\_only}: Only assertion-point actual/expected pairs.
\item \texttt{loop\_only}: Only loop compression summaries.
\item \texttt{VOTA\_no\_ae}: VOTA without assertion-point information.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig1_trace_overview}
\caption{Overview of the verdict prediction pipeline. Source code and a test input are executed to produce a full raw trace. Four conditions vary the trace information: \texttt{no\_trace} (75.13\%), \texttt{full\_trace} (81.76\%), VOTA (99.41\%), and \texttt{exception\_only} (99.83\%). Accuracies for Qwen-2.5-7B (seed=42, $N{=}1{,}190$). Appendix~\ref{sec:vota_examples} provides input/output examples.}
\label{fig:trace_overview}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Models and Training}

We evaluate four models spanning three architecture families: \textbf{Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B/1.5B-Instruct} \citep{qwen2025coder}, \textbf{CodeLlama-13B-Instruct} \citep{roziere2024code}, and \textbf{DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B-Instruct} \citep{deepseek2024coder}, covering a 1.5B--13B parameter range (8.7$\times$).
All conditions use LoRA \citep{hu2022lora} (rank=16, $\alpha$=32, dropout=0.05) on all linear layers, 3 epochs, effective batch size 32, LR $2 \times 10^{-4}$ with linear warmup/decay, max sequence length 2{,}048 tokens.
Sequences exceeding this limit are truncated while preserving the verdict label.
The primary seed is 42; seeds 123 and 456 test \texttt{exception\_only} robustness (Appendix~\ref{sec:training_details}).

\subsection{Loss Ablation}

To disentangle training signal from input signal quality, we introduce \texttt{full\_trace\_label\_only}: identical full-trace input but loss computed only on the verdict token (trace positions masked).
Improvement over standard \texttt{full\_trace} is attributable to \emph{gradient dilution}; the remaining gap to VOTA reflects \emph{information overload} at inference time.

\section{Discussion}

The central finding is that information density determines learning efficiency for trace-based verdict prediction.
Exception events concentrate the runtime's primary fault signal---error type, stack frame, and message---in a compact format (median 150 tokens), while non-exception components (variable states, loop iterations, control flow) record \emph{how} the program ran, not \emph{whether} it failed.

This density perspective explains three key results: (1)~adding trace information beyond exceptions provides no benefit (VOTA $<$ \texttt{exception\_only}, Table~\ref{tab:ablation}), because low-density content competes for attention; (2)~a budget sweep reveals a phase transition at 384$\to$512 tail tokens (+11.0--22.0pp), matching the boundary where diagnostic events dominate, with \texttt{tail\_2048} capturing 95--98\% of \texttt{exception\_only}; (3)~the 6.22pp SFT advantage over regex concentrates on caught-exception boundary cases, confirming semantic signal extraction.
The 0.56pp cross-model gap supports that signal density, not model capacity, is the binding constraint.

\paragraph{Why exception\_only outperforms ae\_only.}
Although \texttt{ae\_only} is a strict superset, multi-seed evaluation reveals that extra content introduces noise: \texttt{ae\_only} achieves 99.64\%$\pm$0.49 while \texttt{exception\_only} achieves 99.83\%$\pm$0.00, indicating that \texttt{exception\_only} captures a minimal sufficient signal with zero seed variance.

\paragraph{SFT vs.\ regex: semantic understanding.}
A regex baseline (B5) achieves 99.33\% by exploiting a format marker, but a \emph{nomarker} ablation (\S\ref{sec:nomarker}) resolves this concern: removing markers drops accuracy by only ${\sim}$2pp (Qwen-7B 97.65\%, Qwen-1.5B 97.82\%), leaving a 4.04pp gap over the best non-trivial regex (93.61\%).
On 8 boundary samples where pass-labeled code triggers exceptions, SFT classifies 6/8 correctly vs.\ 0/8 for regex (McNemar $p{=}0.041$), confirming ${\sim}$91\% of the signal is semantic.

\paragraph{Why zero-shot and few-shot prompting fall short.}
Zero-shot prompting spans 51.0--93.5\% (42.44pp range), which SFT compresses to 0.56pp; three-shot ICL (90.84\%) improves marginally, confirming the bottleneck is verdict calibration rather than exception recognition.

\paragraph{Near-perfect floor, not hard ceiling.}
The 2 errors (both \texttt{GeneratorExit}; Appendix~\ref{sec:error_overlap}) are shared across configurations; CodeLlama-13B seed 123 achieves 100\%, confirming 99.83\% is a convergence \emph{floor}, signal-limited rather than capacity-limited.

\paragraph{Tokenizer efficiency.}
CodeLlama's tokenizer produces ${\sim}$20\% more tokens than Qwen's on identical inputs; a label-only loss variant recovers accuracy at tail-1024 but collapses at tail-2048, indicating LoRA long-sequence instability (Appendix~\ref{sec:tokenizer_analysis}).

\paragraph{Tier hierarchy and methodological implications.}
The two-tier structure (Tier~1 ${\geq}$99\%; Tier~2 = 86.1\%) suggests a fallback design: extract exception events first (median 150 tokens), resort to Tier~2 only when no exception is raised (+10.50pp over no-trace).
Beyond the fallback design, the methodological insight---that information density determines learning efficiency---transfers to any trace-based task where compact diagnostic events exist within verbose traces, such as re-analyzing archived CI/CD logs when the original test environment is unavailable.

\paragraph{Exception events capture test verdict signals, not just crashes.}
Although 188/339 fail samples (55.5\%) are silent logic errors, \texttt{exception\_only} achieves 100\% fail recall because test assertions generate \texttt{AssertionError} events; \texttt{no\_trace} and \texttt{full\_trace} achieve only 62.8\% and 50.0\% fail recall on silent logic errors, confirming ``exception events'' encompasses both crashes and assertion failures.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{training_dynamics}
\caption{Training dynamics comparison. Exception-only (blue) converges to 99.33\% within 50 steps and saturates at 99.83\% by step 150. Full-trace with label-only loss (red, dashed) oscillates between 82\% and 89\% across all 444 training steps without stable convergence, consistent with overfitting to spurious patterns in verbose trace content.}
\label{fig:training_dynamics}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Information density and learning dynamics.}
Training dynamics provide direct evidence that information density determines learning efficiency (Figure~\ref{fig:training_dynamics}).
\texttt{exception\_only} reaches 99.33\% at step 50 (${\sim}$11\% of training) and saturates at 99.83\% by step 150, while \texttt{full\_trace} with label-only loss oscillates between 82\% and 89\% throughout---accuracy drops 5.3pp at step 100, recovers, then drops 3.5pp at steps 250--300---with train loss reaching 0.0085 while eval stalls at 88\%, confirming overfitting (Appendix~\ref{sec:training_dynamics}).
Seed variance negatively correlates with density ($\sigma{=}$0.00/0.38/1.12 for exception/VOTA/label-only), confirming compact inputs stabilize learning.

